# Exact GEMM SASS extraction

Before this fix, `tuning/profile_gemm.py` ran `cuobjdump --dump-sass binary` without an architecture or function filter. For the two global FFN reports analyzed on October 3 this saved 2,212,630,074 and 2,243,266,617 bytes. The existing generic `dump_selected_sass` filters by architecture but still chooses every template instantiation containing a short kernel name; that is broader than the launched kernel.

After the initial draft and review, the root agent activated the profiler and helper fixes in `tuning/profile_gemm.py` and `tuning/sass_exact.py`; all 15 active CPU tests passed. These retained drafts now match the complete architecture-token check and archived-binary extraction:

- [sass_exact.py.draft](../tuning/sass_exact.py.draft) obtains `IAction.NameBase_MANGLED` and measured compute-capability major/minor from the full NCU report. It deduplicates replayed actions, keeps distinct template symbols, and supplies the exact symbols plus the measured `--gpu-architecture` value to cuobjdump. It streams the output to verify that no other architecture or function slipped through, including suffixed architecture tokens such as `sm_90a` and `sm_100f`. Missing identity fails instead of silently dumping everything. Binary, report, extractor and SASS hashes are recorded. It never imports Torch or queries a GPU.
- [profile_gemm_exact_sass.patch.draft](../tuning/profile_gemm_exact_sass.patch.draft) records the import and final-extraction changes relative to the original profiler. It reloads the full report using the installed NCU Python API, preserving multi-kernel split/epilogue captures, and extracts from `run / "harness" / binary.name` so a later installed-binary replacement cannot change the archive being disassembled. The active profiler already includes these changes and imports `tuning.sass_exact`.
- [test_sass_exact.py.draft](../tests/test_sass_exact.py.draft) has 15 passing CPU cases for exact names versus prefixes, extra/missing/duplicate functions, wrong/mixed/missing architectures including `sm_90a` and `sm_100f`, multiple captured symbols, duplicate replays and malformed report identity.

The helper was exercised against both archived binaries/reports. It returned exactly the launched SM120 specialization, with extracts of **156,877 and 290,113 bytes**, and matched the NCU register/shared-memory counts. These outputs are `analysis/sass_exact_sm120.txt` beside each report. The original broad dump was left untouched. Cuobjdump can print a not-found warning for unrelated fatbin translation units; correctness is checked against the final function/architecture set rather than the presence of that warning.

Reproduce from the project directory with the installed CUDA 13.4 cuobjdump and NCU 2026.3 Python API:

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe tools\audit_gemm_profiles.py.draft
Copy-Item -LiteralPath tests\test_sass_exact.py.draft -Destination outputs\test_sass_exact_draft_cpu.py
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest outputs\test_sass_exact_draft_cpu.py -q
```

The test copy lives outside the active test suite and retains the same contents. The initial draft passed 13 cases; the updated draft passes 15 with both architecture-suffix regressions included. Unrelated stale Windows pytest temporary-directory cleanup emitted warnings without affecting the assertions. The audit script writes exact SASS, resource output, static instruction/sample summaries, commands and SHA-256 provenance beside each archived report. It performs no GPU work or extension import. Existing archived extractor hashes describe the version used for their original extraction and are not rewritten by this documentation update.

NCU duration comparisons remain separate from this extraction fix. The current-policy FFN profile used a 2.014 GHz SM clock and the larger-tile profile a 2.465 GHz clock; their 106.752/84.224 µs cold durations are not a controlled resident speed ratio. Neither profile passes the elapsed-peak 85% gate.
